  {
   "id": "uAeuTGSaAwAJ",
   "from": "Terrence Jorgensen",
   "author": "3fe90052fbf2f899",
   "date": "2020-03-21T14:24:04Z",
   "subject": "Re: lavaanify labeling problem?",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div style=\"font-size:small\">I think I might have ran into a problem in the labelling of variables in the lavaanify function. </div></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Nope</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div style=\"font-size:small\">As you can see from the snapshot below it labels parameters 4,5,6 as variances when all other lavInspect outputs labels them as intercepts. By looking at the vcov and coef outputs, lavInspect with what =&quot;free&quot; seems to be correct. Can you check? </div></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">lavInspect() </font>is operating on a fitted model, in which your variables were identified as ordered. <font face=\"courier new, monospace\"> lavaanify() </font>does not know this, so it does not include their thresholds, and also assumes their variances can be freed.</div><div><br></div><div><div><div>Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div>Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics</div><div>Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div><a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792422252978000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2HF5Mq_QjQa1nD71KMZVCv\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div></div><div><br></div><div></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
